    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In the development of cancer, tumor angiogenesis is vital for tumor development as new blood vessel formation supplies the growing tumor with oxygen and nutrients. MicroRNAs (miRNAs) are small non-coding endogenous RNA molecules that play a critical role in regulating angiogenesis and are also often dysregulated in cancer, making them potential therapeutic targets. Our lab previously reported that deletion of microRNA-126 (miR-126), the most abundant of all miRNAs found in endothelial cells, leads to impaired angiogenesis and defective vascular integrity in embryonic mice as manifested by vascular leakage, edema, hemorrhaging, and decreased retinal vascularization. In addition, we previously showed that miR-126 positively modulates angiogenesis by suppressing negative regulators of signaling pathways downstream of the vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) pathway, one of the most important stimulators of angiogenesis. Here, we explored the role of miR-126 in tumor angiogenesis by utilizing the B16 melanoma syngeneic tumor model for mice. We found that tumors in miR-126 knockout mice displayed smaller volume size, decreased vessel density, and greater necrosis area. However, miR-126 deletion increased the incidence rate of metastatic tumor foci found in the lungs. We also performed in vitro studies affirming the mechanism by which miR-126 regulates angiogenesis, showing that miR-126 inhibition significantly increases Spred1 and p85β gene expression in human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVECs). Because Spred1 and p85β normally suppress the VEGF-dependent MAPK and PI3K signaling pathways, respectively, inhibition of miR-126 results in the disruption of angiogenesis. Thus, our findings suggest that miR-126 acts as an important regulator of tumor growth and tumor angiogenesis in melanoma cancer. However, before miR-126 can further be considered as an anti-angiogenic target in the treatment of cancers, additional studies should be done to reconcile why miR-126 deletion in vivo leads to increased lung metastases despite reduced tumor vessel density.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
